{% capture tags %}
[
  {%- for item in data.tags -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Kept for tags.json files published by OLDER platform versions, which is where a blank
      entry can still come from: the generator used to serialize a class with no `@tag_name` as
      `""`, and this line is what made the resulting gap invisible. Current builds cannot
      produce one — the generator raises, and `verify_tags_json.rb` fails the build against the
      tag registry — so this is compatibility with what CloudFront already holds, not a guard
      against today's generator.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% if item.name == blank %}{% continue %}{% endif %}
    {
      "category": "{{ item.category | default: 'platformOS' }}",
      {%- comment -%}
        Read through under the names they are published as; the `deprecated_*` fallback serves a
        tags.json from a platform version before the generator emitted `deprecation_*`.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecated": {{ item.deprecated | default: false | json }},
      "deprecation_reason": {{ item.deprecation_reason | default: item.deprecated_reason | default: '' | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        The successor's registered NAME: platformos-check renames a deprecated tag to it and, under
        `pos-cli check run -a`, writes the result unattended.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecation_replacement": {{ item.deprecation_replacement | default: item.deprecated_replacement | default: '' | json }},
      "description": {{ item.description | json }},
      "syntax": {{ item.syntax | json }},
      "platformOS": true,
      "name": "{{item.name}}",
      {%- comment -%}
        The TYPES the generator published, passed through — the same treatment
        `platformos_filters.liquid` gives a filter argument, and for the same reason: this file is
        a transport, not an author.

        It used to emit the literal `["untyped"]` for every tag argument, and that one line was the
        whole reason 67 of the 72 published tag parameters carried no type while every `@param` in
        `app/lib/liquify/tags/*.rb` upstream already declared one. `{% cache 'k', expire: 20 %}`
        annotates `expire [number]`; a consumer reading this endpoint was told it could be anything.
        platformos-check's `ValidTagArgumentTypes` reads exactly this field and reports nothing for
        `untyped`, so the effect was a check that could never fire.

        `{% if %}`, not `| default:`, matching the filters partial: an empty array is a legitimate
        published value and `default` would substitute for it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "parameters": [
        {% for param in item.params %}
          {
          "description": {{ param.description | json }},
            "name": {{ param.name | json }},
            "required": {{ param.required | default: false | json }},
            "types": {% if param.types %}{{ param.types | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %},
            "array_value": {{ param.array_value | default: '' | json }}
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      {%- comment -%}
        The same element shape a filter's return type is published in — `type`, singular, as a
        string — so a consumer has one shape to read rather than two.

        The tag generator emits `returns` now, from a `@return` annotation on the handler class,
        and only for the tags whose return the implementation guarantees. A tag nobody has
        established one for loops zero times here and publishes `[]`, which reads as "the
        documentation does not say" — never as "no return type".

        This is what platformos-check reads to know that `{% graphql g = … %}` leaves a Hash in
        `g`. While every entry was empty it carried its own measured table of four tags instead.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "return_type": [
        {% for return_type in item.returns %}
          {
            "type": "{% print return_type.types | join: ', ' | downcase %}",
            "name": "",
            "description": {{ return_type.description | json }},
            "array_value": ""
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      "examples": [
        {% for example in item.examples %}
          {
            "name": "",
            "description": "",
            "syntax": "",
            "path": "",
            "raw_liquid": {{ example | json }},
            "parameter": false,
            "display_type": "text",
            "show_data_tab": true
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ]
    }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
  {% endfor %}
]
{% endcapture %}
{% liquid
  assign tags = tags | parse_json: raw_text: true
  return tags
%}
